+{"id":"a97cec02-6cb5-4d07-950a-b4595408498b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:05:00.179610","source":"agent","parent_id":"38025cdb-c254-4b73-8610-4a2636939aac","thought":[],"reasoning_content":"Now let me look at the `check_anchors` command more closely. Looking at lines 383-428 in `record.py`, I see the `cmd_check_anchors` function. Let me analyze it:\n\n1. It loads the chain\n2. It iterates through anchor files in the anchors directory\n3. For each anchor:\n   - If it's an OTS proof (`m.group(2) == \"ots\"`), it calls `check_ots(path, raw, a.explorer)`\n   - If it's a sigstore attestation, it calls `check_sigstore(path, ...)`\n4. It collects errors in `bad` and `cant` lists\n5. At the end, it prints JSON and error messages to stderr\n6. It returns `1 if bad else 2 if cant else 0`\n\nThe bug is that when an OTS proof file is deleted (missing), the code still processes the sigstore attestation but not the OTS proof. Looking at the logic:\n\n- When a file exists and is processed, it gets added to `rows`\n- When there's an error (Bad or CantCheck), it gets added to `bad` or `cant` lists\n\nThe issue is that when an OTS file is missing, the `_listdir` function (lines 169-182) should not even list it because it checks for file existence. Let me check `_listdir`:\n\n```python\ndef _listdir(ledger, sub, pattern, what):\n    d = os.path.join(ledger, sub)\n    if not os.path.exists(d):\n        return []\n    if os.path.islink(d) or not os.path.isdir(d):\n        raise Bad(f\"{sub}/ must be a plain directory\")\n    out = []\n    for n in sorted(os.listdir(d)):\n        p = os.path.join(d, n)\n        if not pattern.fullmatch(n) or os.path.islink(p) or not os.path.isfile(p):\n            raise Bad(f\"{sub}/{n!r}: unexpected {what}\")\n        out.append(p)\n    return out\n```\n\nThe `_listdir` function checks if each file is a regular file using `os.path.isfile(p)`. This means that broken or missing files won't pass this check. However, the current implementation doesn't handle the case where a file should exist but doesn't.\n\nThe fix needs to:\n1. Check that the `.ots` file exists for every range head that has a `.sigstore.json` attestation\n2. Print a message to stderr containing `NNNNNN.ots is missing` for each missing proof\n3. Exit with code 1 if any `.ots` proof is missing\n4. Ensure existing tests still pass\n\nLet me run the tests to understand the current state:","thinking_blocks":[],"action":{"command":"cd /workspace/record && python3 -m unittest test_record -v","is_input":false,"reset":false,"kind":"TerminalAction"},"tool_name":"terminal","tool_call_id":"terminal_h391fbk6v944","tool_call":{"id":"terminal_h391fbk6v944","name":"terminal","arguments":"{\"command\": \"cd /workspace/record && python3 -m unittest test_record -v\"}","origin":"completion"},"llm_response_id":"gen-1794384297-84ET0fRux0vSnBV9Ua3U","security_risk":"UNKNOWN","summary":"terminal: {\"command\": \"cd /workspace/record && python3 -m unittest test_record -v\"}","kind":"ActionEvent"}
