+{"id":"2c8cf286-fab4-4146-baa1-841a78e55ac7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:05:04.569301","source":"agent","parent_id":"9163e99d-e637-45f5-b68b-c1fd36c6c1e1","thought":[],"reasoning_content":"I need to understand the issue better. Let me look at the `cmd_check_anchors` function more carefully to see how it currently handles OTS proofs.\n\nLooking at the code around line 383-428, I see that `cmd_check_anchors`:\n1. Loads the chain\n2. Iterates through anchors in the anchors directory\n3. For each anchor, it checks if it's a .ots or .sigstore.json file\n4. For .ots files, it calls `check_ots()` which reads the file\n5. For .sigstore.json files, it validates the Sigstore bundle\n\nThe bug is that if an OTS proof file is deleted (e.g., `anchors/000002.ots` is missing but `anchors/000002.sigstore.json` exists), the code doesn't detect this as a missing file.\n\nLooking at the loop at line 388:\n```python\nfor path in _listdir(a.ledger, \"anchors\", ANCHOR_NAME, \"file in anchors\"):\n```\n\nThis only lists files that exist in the anchors directory. If a file doesn't exist, it won't be in the directory listing at all.\n\nSo the fix needs to:\n1. Check what OTS files should exist based on the sigstore attestations\n2. Verify that those files actually exist\n3. Report missing ones\n\nLet me examine the structure more carefully. The sigstore files contain attestations that cover ranges of entries. For each sigstore attestation, there should be a corresponding .ots file.\n\nLet me look for how to identify which OTS files should exist.","thinking_blocks":[],"action":{"command":"view","path":"/workspace/record/record.py","view_range":[383,430],"kind":"FileEditorAction"},"tool_name":"file_editor","tool_call_id":"file_editor_6n5p9ds2zwz2","tool_call":{"id":"file_editor_6n5p9ds2zwz2","name":"file_editor","arguments":"{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/workspace/record/record.py\", \"view_range\": [383, 430]}","origin":"completion"},"llm_response_id":"gen-1794384302-4hiGw5QFTFxy4jWb